<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Taromai</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. <a href="/years/1429">1429</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Tarumi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Taromai was the last king of Nanzan, the southern of the three kingdoms that divided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okinawa-island">Okinawa Island</a> in the 14th and early 15th centuries. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sho-hashi">Shō Hashi</a> of Chuzan conquered Nanzan, completing the unification of Okinawa as the Ryukyu Kingdom: the later official history dates this to 1429, the year of Taromai's last recorded mission to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, while the earlier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chuzan-seikan">Chūzan Seikan</a> puts the fall of Nanzan before 1421.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/taromai.jpg" alt="Taromai" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kugel · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tomb_of_Tarumi_01.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Taromai, whose name is also read Tarumoi and Tarumī, is known chiefly from Chinese records, which gave him the title of King of Sannan. He first sent to the Chinese emperor in 1415, presenting himself as heir to King Ōōso without saying clearly how he was related to him. By his report, Ōōso had been killed by his elder brother Tabuchi, who was in turn killed by local chiefs in favour of Crown Prince Taromai.</p><p>The account was unusual, since Okinawan rulers routinely led the Chinese to believe that the throne passed from father to son, and the historian Dana Masayuki has suggested it may have covered an illegitimate seizure of power. His last recorded contact with the Chinese court was in 1429. His name is also unusual in the Chinese records, as it seems to be a genuine Okinawan given name, Taru-mi: Tarū was a common name, and -mi a suffix used by the ruling elite.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Later Okinawan histories knew the King of Sannan as the Aji, or local ruler, of Ōzato, but did not know how many men had held the title. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chuzan-seikan">Chūzan Seikan</a> of 1650 tells of an unnamed King of Sannan who lost the support of his people and was overthrown by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sho-hashi">Shō Hashi</a>, then Aji of Sashiki, and Sai Taku's edition of the Chūzan Seifu of 1701 largely followed it.</p><p>Sai On's edition of the Chūzan Seifu of 1725 changed the story. Drawing on Chinese diplomatic records, he added the tribute missions sent in Taromai's name, named Taromai as the last King of Sannan and placed the fall of Sannan in 1429, after the last mission. To fit this to the older narrative he had to divide Ōzato in two, treating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sho-hashi">Shō Hashi</a>'s early conquest as that of the Aji of Shimasoe-Ōzato and the King of Sannan as the Aji of Shimajiri-Ōzato. Diplomatic documents of Taromai are preserved in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rekidai-hoan">Rekidai Hōan</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Taromai” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Sho Hashi, who had taken control of Chuzan, conquered Nanzan in 1429, defeating its last king <b>Taromai</b> and uniting Okinawa as the Ryukyu Kingdom.</blockquote><a href="/state/nanzan">Nanzan</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okinawa-prefecture">Okinawa Prefecture</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=26.21,127.68&amp;year=1429" aria-label="Okinawa Prefecture on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Taromai"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5588 5304h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5588 5304h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=26.21,127.68&amp;year=1429">What was here in 1429? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
